:: warrior_queen_day_1_1

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
   DAY 1 — THE RECKONING IN THE IRON GARDEN
   Part 1: The Summons
═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

The summons came before dawn.

A single word scratched onto parchment in Aldric's brutal hand, delivered under my chamber door by a servant who had the good sense to knock once and flee: //Come.//

I groaned, rolled over — and felt it.

The sheets dragged across my chest, and two points of sharp, bright sensitivity flared beneath the linen like sparks struck from flint. I froze, one hand braced against the mattress, the other hovering over the left side of my chest where the sensation bloomed hottest. The nipple was... tender. Not the flat, ignorable nub it had been all my life, but something raised, swollen, exquisitely aware of the fabric sliding across it.

I pressed my fingers there, gently. The flesh beneath yielded — soft, warm, a small mound of new tissue that had not been there a fortnight past. Not large. Not yet anything anyone would call a breast. But //present//. Present enough that the roughness of linen felt like a tongue dragging over a fresh burn.

<<speech "mc">>Damn you, Thalara,<</speech>> I muttered to the ceiling.

The ceiling offered no sympathy. Ceilings seldom do.

I swung my legs from the bed and stood — and the world tilted by a degree so subtle I might have imagined it, were it not for the faint pull of unfamiliar weight at my chest and the way my hips, wider this week than last, shifted the axis of my balance as I rose. I steadied myself against the bedpost. The wood was cold against a palm that was smoother than it should have been — calluses thinning, the skin beneath them finer-grained, as though someone were slowly sanding away the prince and revealing something underneath.

I crossed to the wardrobe. My old training leathers hung inside — the chest piece, the bracers, the trousers, the boots. I had worn them a hundred times. A thousand. They smelled of sweat and oil and the Iron Garden's particular blend of dust and blood. They smelled like //me//.

Or they had. Once.

[[Dress for training|warrior_queen_day_1_2]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_2

I pulled the trousers on first. They fit — mostly. The waist was slightly loose where my midsection had narrowed, but the hips... the hips were snug. Not painfully so, but the leather that had once slid up without resistance now required a shimmy, a tug, and a muttered curse as I worked the material over hip bones that were migrating outward by millimetres each day. The seat of the trousers was tighter too, the leather pulling across a backside that was — gods help me — //rounder// than it had been.

I laced the waist and ignored the way the trousers now hugged curves that a prince's trousers were not designed to hug.

The chest piece came next. And this was where the morning's first true battle occurred.

The leather breastplate was flat. Designed for a male chest — for the broad, hard plane of pectoral muscle that I had maintained through years of training. I lifted it, pressed it against my torso, and began to lace the sides.

The leather sat on the budding swells of my chest like a plank laid over two stones. The pressure was immediate and unpleasant — the stiff hide compressing tender, swollen tissue against my ribs, the new nipples grinding against leather that offered no give, no accommodation, no acknowledgment that there was something //there// now that needed space.

I laced tighter, wincing. If I could just flatten them — press the new flesh back into the chest wall — perhaps they would behave. Perhaps I could pretend, for one more morning, that the body beneath this armour was the body I had always known.

The left nipple sent a jolt of protest through my ribs that made me gasp.

I stopped lacing. Breathed through my teeth. Adjusted the chest piece a fraction to the left, then the right, searching for a position that did not feel like wearing a punishment.

There was no such position. The breasts — //budding// breasts, I corrected myself savagely, they were barely anything — refused to be ignored.

<<speech "mc">>Fine,<</speech>> I told them, as though they were listening. <<speech "mc">>But you shall behave yourself in the yard, or I swear by every god that ever drew breath, I shall bind you with rope and leather until you are flat as flagstone.<</speech>>

They offered no reply. But the left one throbbed gently, as if in disagreement.

[[Head to the Iron Garden|warrior_queen_day_1_3]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_3

The corridor outside my chamber was empty — the castle's servants knew better than to linger near the heir's quarters before dawn. But as I passed the junction with the maid's corridor, a door opened and Maera's sharp, dark-curled head emerged.

She took one look at me — leathers, sword belt, hair haveily knotted, the expression of a person being dragged to their own execution — and leaned against the doorframe with her arms folded.

<<speech "maera">>Training leathers. Before dawn. You look like a kicked dog someone has put into armour.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric summoned me.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Mmmhm. And you are wearing the old chest piece.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>It is the only chest piece I have.<</speech>>

She pushed off the doorframe and stepped into the corridor. Her eyes — quick, dark, merciless in the way that only a childhood friend's eyes can be — dropped to my chest. Specifically, to the way the flat leather breastplate sat on what was beneath it. She did not stare. She //assessed//.

<<speech "maera">>It does not fit you anymore.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>It fits fine.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Caelan. I can see the shape of your nipples pressing through the leather. If I can see them from here, every soldier in the yard will see them from there.<</speech>>

Heat rushed up my neck and into my face like a flame climbing a wick. I looked down — and she was right. The new nipples, larger and more prominent than they had been even a week ago, were pressing outward against the flat leather, creating two small, unmistakable points that no amount of lacing could conceal.

<<speech "mc">>I... did not notice.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Men never do. Not until someone else notices for them.<</speech>> She said it without cruelty — with the wry, matter-of-fact tone of a woman stating an obvious truth. <<speech "maera">>Here.<</speech>>

She disappeared into her chamber and returned a moment later with a strip of thick, soft linen — the kind used for binding wounds. Without asking permission, she stepped close and began folding the linen into a wide band.

<<speech "maera">>Lift your arms.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Maera, I do not need —<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Lift. Your. Arms.<</speech>>

I lifted my arms. She reached around me, passed the linen band across my chest — directly over the sensitive buds — and pulled it snug. The fabric compressed the new tissue against my ribs, not painfully, but firmly. The nipples, padded by the linen's thickness, were no longer grinding against the rough leather. The small mounds of flesh were pressed flat — not invisible, but less obvious. Less //present//.

<<speech "maera">>There,<</speech>> she said, tucking the end of the band. Her hands were quick and impersonal, a maid's hands performing a practical task. <<speech "maera">>That will hold for the morning. When you are done being beaten by Aldric, come find me. We shall talk about getting you a proper binding.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>A binding?<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Aye. Like the ones washerwomen use when their breasts are sore from milk. Or like the ones soldiers' wives use when they ride long distances. Or —<</speech>> she met my eyes with a gentleness that cut deeper than any blade <<speech "maera">>— like the one any woman wears who must fight in armour not built for her body.<</speech>>

//Any woman.//

The words hung in the dim corridor like dust motes in a sunbeam.

<<speech "mc">>I am not —<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Go,<</speech>> she said softly. <<speech "maera">>He is waiting. And Caelan?<</speech>>

I paused at the corridor's turn.

<<speech "maera">>The binding is not shame. It is armour. Remember that.<</speech>>

I said nothing. I walked toward the Iron Garden with a strip of maid's linen binding my chest, and the warmth of her hands lingered on my ribs like a brand.

[[Enter the Iron Garden|warrior_queen_day_1_4]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_4

The Iron Garden lay wreathed in grey mist when I arrived. The dawn had not yet committed itself to the sky — the light was pewter, uncertain, and the cobblestones were slick with dew that would burn off within the hour.

Sir Aldric Vornhelm stood at the yard's centre like a boulder that had grown weary of the mountain and walked down to kill something.

He was everything a warrior was supposed to be. Broad-shouldered. Thick-armed. Scarred across the jaw and temple and forearms, each mark a record of violence survived. His grey hair was pulled back in a tight knot. Frostfang — that monstrous broadsword, five feet of castle-forged steel — hung at his hip as naturally as another man's hand hung at his side. His arms were folded. His face betrayed absolutely nothing.

He did not greet me. He did not acknowledge my arrival. He tossed me a longsword — the same blade I had trained with since the age of twelve, twenty-eight inches of familiar steel — and said:

<<speech "aldric">>Strike me.<</speech>>

I caught the blade, settled into the wide-legged power stance that had been carved into my bones through a thousand drills, and lunged.

He disarmed me in three strokes.

Not through superior strength — though he possessed that in terrible abundance. Through //geometry//. My thrust overextended by two inches. My shoulders, narrower now than they had been even a moon ago, could not generate the same rotational force behind the blade. The lunge that should have driven the point toward his belly drifted left, pulled by a centre of gravity that had migrated //downward// — into hips that were wider, a pelvis that was quietly, stubbornly reshaping itself beneath skin and sinew.

My leading foot landed wrong. My weight pitched forward. And Frostfang's flat slapped the blade from my grip with contemptuous ease, sending it ringing across the cobblestones like a bell tolling for a funeral.

I stared at my empty hands.

The palms were softer than they should have been. The calluses — built over years of gripping steel and leather — were thinning, the ridges smoothing, the skin beneath them finer-grained and pale. The fingers were longer. More tapered. The knuckles less pronounced. A woman's hands growing inside a swordsman's gloves.

Even my grip was betraying me.

[[Try again|warrior_queen_day_1_5]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_5

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

I retrieved the sword. Set the stance. Drove forward with everything the old body knew how to give.

Disarmed in four moves. A marginal improvement that tasted like ashes.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

We drilled for two hours in that grey dawn. Every combination I had mastered since boyhood misfired.

The //overhead cleave// — my finest stroke, the one that had split practice dummies from crown to groin, the blow that had made Aldric nod in approval three years past — pulled savagely to the left. The new weight on my chest, small as it was, shifted during the downswing. The binding Maera had wrapped compressed the tissue but could not eliminate its mass. Two small mounds of flesh, each barely larger than an egg, and yet they moved — shifted — and dragged the blade's arc off true by inches that might as well have been miles.

The //pivot-and-slash//, a manoeuvre I could once execute in my sleep, wobbled and died on its feet. My hips were wider. The rotation that once spun on a narrow male pelvis now ground against a broader axis, and the footwork — the footwork I had drilled ten thousand times — could not account for bones that were moving, spreading, becoming something the footwork had never been designed for.

The //rising parry// failed because my wrists lacked the snap they'd had a month ago — the tendons looser, the joint more flexible, the grip that needed to lock rigid instead //giving//, absorbing force in a way that was perhaps useful for something but was categorically useless for this.

I grew furious. Not at Aldric — at this treacherous flesh that would not obey.

At the soft swelling beneath the binding that bounced when I lunged and ached when I twisted, despite Maera's careful wrapping. At the hair that fell into my eyes, longer now and finer, slipping free of the tie I had knotted at my nape. At the way my thighs rubbed together at the top during footwork — a friction that had never existed before the hips began to spread and the flesh there began to thicken.

At the way my voice cracked between registers when I cursed — neither the prince's baritone nor whatever it was becoming.

<<speech "mc">>DAMN this useless — !<</speech>>

The curse came out wrong. The first syllable was the prince's voice, low and hard. The second was something higher — not feminine, not yet, but //displaced//, as though the vocal cords were a string being slowly retuned and the pitch was wandering between notes.

Aldric's eyebrows did not move. His expression did not change. But I saw him hear it. I saw the flicker of recognition in those grey eyes.

He had heard my voice crack. He had heard the prince's baritone stumble and the beginnings of something else peek through the gap.

I wanted to die.

[[Aldric calls a halt|warrior_queen_day_1_6]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_6

<<speech "aldric">>Enough.<</speech>>

He sat upon a weapons rack, arms folded — always folded, as though keeping his hands occupied would prevent them from doing something violent to the nearest inanimate object. He regarded me with those storm-grey eyes that had watched men die on six battlefields.

I stood panting, dripping sweat, the longsword hanging from a grip that trembled with exhaustion and shame. The binding around my chest was damp and had loosened during the drills — I could feel the small mounds of new flesh shifting beneath it, no longer fully compressed, pressing against the leather chest piece with renewed insistence.

A long silence. The kind of silence Aldric wielded like a weapon — he could outwait stone.

<<speech "aldric">>Your fighting style was built upon a frame that no longer exists.<</speech>>

He said it as though remarking upon the weather.

<<speech "aldric">>The shoulders that drove your cleave are narrowing. Half an inch since last month, perhaps more. Your reach has shortened with them. The hips that anchored your stance are widening — I can see it in the footwork, the way the rotation catches where once it flowed. The chest that braced your guard now carries weight it was never shaped to bear.<</speech>>

He paused. Let the silence do its work.

<<speech "aldric">>The hands are softer. The grip weaker by measurable degrees. The wrists more flexible, which will be useful eventually but is a liability now. Your center of gravity has dropped by two inches, perhaps three — the hips and the... chest... are redistributing your mass. The thighs are thickening with new tissue. And your voice —<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>My voice is fine.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Your voice is changing. I heard it crack just now. The baritone is becoming unreliable. Within a fortnight it will be gone entirely, if the potions hold their pace.<</speech>>

He delivered this catalogue of my unravelling with the clinical detachment of an armourer listing damage to a suit of plate. No pity. No revulsion. No embarrassment. Just facts, laid out in a row like blades on a rack.

I gripped the longsword so hard my knuckles ached. The softer knuckles. The thinner fingers.

<<speech "aldric">>If you do continue to fight as a man in a body that is becoming a woman's, you shall die. Not in training. On a field. With a blade in your belly and surprise upon your face, because the stroke that should have saved you pulled left by an inch and the foot that should have held firm slipped on a hip that was wider than your muscle memory expected.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Then what would you have me do?<</speech>>

The words came through clenched teeth. I tasted copper — I had bitten the inside of my cheek during one of the failed drills and not noticed until now.

Aldric rose from the weapons rack. He walked toward me — not fast, not slow, with the measured gait of a man who had never once been in a hurry because the world had learned to wait for him.

He stopped an arm's length away. Close enough that I could see the individual scars, the silver stubble, the deep lines carved by decades of squinting into sun and rain and the faces of enemies.

<<speech "aldric">>Tomorrow, we begin anew. From nothing.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>From nothing? I have trained since I was twelve —<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>The boy who trained since twelve is gone. His body is gone. His reach, his strength, his balance, his centre — gone. Every technique you have mastered was built for a structure that is being dismantled. I cannot rebuild upon a foundation that is sinking. So I shall clear the ground and lay a new one.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>A new foundation. For what?<</speech>>

His grey eyes held mine. Something moved in them — not warmth, exactly, but a kind of ferocious, guarded respect. The respect of a man who would not lie to you, even when the truth was a blade.

<<speech "aldric">>For whatever you are becoming.<</speech>>

He turned and walked away. Frostfang did not even rattle at his hip. The man moved like the weapon was part of him — an extension of a body he had never once been forced to doubt.

I stood alone in the Iron Garden and watched his shadow shrink across the cobblestones.

[[Stay in the yard|warrior_queen_day_1_7]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_7

I did not leave.

The mist burned off and the sun climbed the eastern wall, and I stayed. I swung the longsword at nothing — at shadows, at air, at the ghost of the swordsman I had been — and felt every stroke drift and pull and fail as the body beneath the blade continued its quiet rebellion.

At one point I stopped and stood still, breathing hard, and became aware of something I had been refusing to notice.

The binding had slipped.

During two hours of lunging and slashing and the violent torso rotation of a swordfight, Maera's careful wrapping had worked loose. The linen band had shifted downward, bunching at my ribs rather than covering the chest. Above it, freed from compression, the small swells of my budding breasts sat against my sternum — unbound, unsupported, pressed against the leather chest piece with nothing between them and the rough hide but a sweat-dampened tunic.

I could feel them. Not as pain — though they ached, a low, pervasive throb that pulsed in time with my heartbeat — but as //presence//. As weight. As two soft, warm, undeniable additions to a chest that had been flat for twenty years of my life.

They moved when I breathed. A subtle rise and fall, the tissue expanding and contracting with the ribcage beneath, but //delayed// — the flesh responding to the breath a half-beat behind the bone, like a wave following the wind. When I inhaled deeply, they pressed outward against the leather. When I exhaled, they settled — and the settling itself was a sensation, a gentle shift of weight and warmth that the old chest had never produced.

I raised my hand and, with a self-consciousness that felt absurd given that I was entirely alone, pressed my palm flat against my left chest.

The flesh yielded beneath my hand. Soft. Warm. Dense with the particular quality of tissue that was growing rather than simply existing. Beneath the pad of new breast tissue, I could feel the pectoral muscle — still present, still defined, but overlaid now with something that had no business being there and yet was //there//, unmistakably, and getting more there by the day.

The nipple pressed against my palm. Larger than it had been. Raised. The areola around it wider, the skin there slightly darker, slightly puckered, and so sensitive that the pressure of my hand sent a shiver down my spine that had nothing to do with cold.

I snatched my hand away.

<<speech "mc">>Stop it,<</speech>> I told the breast. <<speech "mc">>Stop... //being// there.<</speech>>

It did not stop being there. It continued being there with the calm persistence of something that had been given a task and intended to see it through regardless of the host's opinions.

I turned to the polished training shield mounted on the yard wall — the one used for checking stance and form — and looked at my reflection.

[[Face the reflection|warrior_queen_day_1_8]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_8

The shield was bronze, polished to a mirror's clarity, and it did not lie.

The figure in the reflection was... between things.

The face was still mine — mostly. The jawline had softened, the hard angles blurring toward something less angular. The cheekbones were higher, or perhaps the cheeks were thinner — the face was losing its squareness, trending toward an oval that was not yet feminine but was no longer definitively male. The brow was smoother. The lips fuller by a shade. The eyes the same — the Velmoria grey, my mother's eyes — but set now in a face that framed them differently. More prominently. More...

//Pretty// was not a word I was willing to use. Not yet.

The hair fell past my shoulders in waves that the old straight, coarse prince's hair had never produced. Thalara's potions had changed even that — the texture finer, the colour richer, the strands catching the morning light with a sheen that belonged on a noble lady's head, not on a warrior standing in a training yard with a sword in hand.

Below the neck, the reflection grew more troubling.

The shoulders were narrower. Visibly, measurably narrower than they had been at the start of the potions. The collarbone was more prominent — a horizontal ridge that caught shadows. The old broad, powerful upper frame that had filled armour and swung swords was //shrinking//, and in its place was something lighter, leaner, more delicate in structure if not yet in appearance.

And below the collarbone — there they were. Even through the leather chest piece, even with the binding bunched uselessly at my ribs, the small swells of the budding breasts created a visible disruption in the chest's silhouette. Two gentle curves, slight but undeniable, that pushed the flat leather outward by a fraction of an inch on each side. Enough to see. Enough for anyone with eyes to look at my reflection and know that the chest beneath that armour was no longer a man's.

The waist was narrower. The hips wider. The trousers hugged curves that should not have been there. Between the hip and the thigh, the profile had changed — rounder, fuller, the straight line of the male body curving inward at the waist and outward at the hip in a shape that was becoming, unmistakably, an hourglass.

I stared at the reflection. The reflection stared back. Neither of us blinked.

<<speech "mc">>Who are you?<</speech>> I asked, quietly.

The reflection offered no answer. Reflections seldom do. But the face in the bronze — that softening, changing, almost-pretty face framed by long hair and set above a body that was growing breasts and hips and curves — looked back at me with my mother's eyes, and for a moment I could not tell whether I was looking at the prince who was dying or the queen who was being born.

A sound behind me. I spun — hand going to the sword, old instincts still sharp even if the body serving them was not — and found Torin.

[[Torin appears|warrior_queen_day_1_9]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_9

He stood at the yard entrance in his black-and-silver armour, his grey eyes fixed on me with the quiet, volcanic intensity that was simply //Torin// — the way water was wet, the way stone was hard.

How long had he been watching?

<<speech "torin">>Your Highness.<</speech>> His voice was low, careful. <<speech "torin">>I heard you training. I came to —<</speech>>

He stopped. His eyes had dropped — involuntarily, I thought, because the faintest colour touched his cheeks when he realised where his gaze had landed. He was looking at my chest. At the visible shape of the budding breasts beneath the ill-fitting leather, the binding loose and useless, the tunic beneath damp with sweat and clinging.

His gaze snapped back to my face with a speed that suggested it had been dragged there by main force.

<<speech "torin">>I came to ensure your safety,<</speech>> he finished, his voice steady as oak despite the flush creeping up his neck.

<<speech "mc">>I am in the training yard, Torin. The most dangerous thing present is a weapons rack.<</speech>>

<<speech "torin">>And a great deal of sharp steel. And you, alone, in a state that might be called... distracted.<</speech>>

He was not wrong. I //had// been distracted. Distracted by a body that insisted on becoming something new. Distracted by breasts that refused to be bound and hips that refused to be hidden and a reflection that refused to show me the prince.

I became suddenly, wretchedly aware of what I must look like. Hair tangled and damp with sweat. Chest piece sitting wrong on a chest that was wrong for it. The shape of my nipples probably visible through the leather — Maera had warned me, and the binding had failed. My face flushed from exertion and, now, from the knowledge that Torin — stoic, devoted, silently burning Torin — had seen all of it.

<<speech "mc">>You may go,<</speech>> I said, too quickly. <<speech "mc">>I am well.<</speech>>

<<speech "torin">>You are not well. You are standing in an empty yard, speaking to your reflection, with a binding slipping down your —<</speech>>

He stopped himself. Swallowed. The flush on his neck deepened.

<<speech "torin">>With a binding that has come loose,<</speech>> he amended, his eyes rigidly fixed on a point approximately two inches above my head. <<speech "torin">>Shall I... summon Maera?<</speech>>

The kindness of the offer — the careful, clumsy, //agonized// kindness of a man who wanted to help and had no framework for helping with //this// — cracked something in my chest that was not the budding breast tissue.

<<speech "mc">>No. Thank you. I shall manage.<</speech>>

He nodded once. Turned to go. Then paused, his armoured back to me.

<<speech "torin">>Your Highness.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aye?<</speech>>

A long silence. The kind of silence that held words prisoner.

<<speech "torin">>The yard... the reflection... what you are becoming... it does not —<</speech>> He struggled. Words were not his weapons. <<speech "torin">>It does not lessen you. In my eyes.<</speech>>

He left before I could respond. His armoured footsteps rang in the corridor and faded, and I stood in the Iron Garden with my loose binding and my budding breasts and the phantom warmth of a devotion that could not speak its own name.

[[Go to the bath|warrior_queen_day_1_10]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_10

The corridor from the Iron Garden to my chambers crossed the main passage, and at midmorning it was not empty.

Servants moved through with linens and trays. Two soldiers — young, newly posted to the castle guard — passed me with salutes that were crisp and correct and accompanied by eyes that lingered a fraction too long. I felt their gazes like fingertips trailing down my back, touching the places where the body had changed — the narrower shoulders, the longer hair, the hips that swayed beneath the leather despite my best efforts to walk as I always had.

I had always walked //hard//. Heel-strike, shoulders square, each step a small conquest of ground. The prince's walk. The soldier's walk. But the body was subtly, continuously negotiating with me, and today the negotiation was about //gait//. The wider hips altered the angle of the femur. The thigh bones, angled inward from a broader pelvis, changed the mechanics of each stride. The result, no matter how fiercely I tried to stamp it out, was a softness to the step — a slight lateral sway, a rolling of the hips that distributed the new weight more efficiently but also looked, from behind, unmistakably...

I caught the younger soldier elbowing his companion. A whispered exchange. A smothered grin.

They were looking at my arse.

I was halfway down the corridor, and two soldiers were staring at the backside of the heir to the throne, and that backside was //rounder// than it should have been, and the leather trousers were //tighter// than they should have been, and the walk was //softer// than I could prevent it from being, and the humiliation of it was so complete and so total that I nearly broke into a run.

Instead I straightened my spine, squared the narrowing shoulders, and walked past them without looking back. The walk was still soft. The hips still swayed. The soldiers still watched.

Some battles cannot be won with steel. Some battles are fought in the space between one step and the next, in the distance between who you were and who the world is beginning to see.

I reached my chambers. Shut the door. Locked it.

And then, alone, I unwound the useless binding from my chest with fingers that shook — and stood in the quiet room with nothing between the budding breasts and the air but a damp tunic, and felt the cool draught from the window find them through the thin linen, and shivered.

[[Draw the bath|warrior_queen_day_1_11]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_11

I could have called a servant to draw the bath. The prince always had.

But the prince had been flat-chested and narrow-hipped, and the idea of a castle servant seeing //this// body — the halfway body, the between body, the body that was growing breasts and widening at the hip and softening in places that softened — was more than I could stomach.

I heated the water myself. Hauled the buckets from the boiler in the corridor, filled the copper tub, tested the temperature with an elbow. Domestic labour. Women's work, the old men of the court would call it. But there was no one here to judge, and the water was hot, and I needed it.

I stripped.

Tunic first. The linen clung to my skin — sweat-damp, reluctant to release — and when I peeled it upward over my head, the fabric dragged across the nipples and they flared with that electric sensitivity that was still new enough to startle me every time. A small sound escaped my lips. Not quite a gasp. Not quite a moan. Something between — a sharp, involuntary intake of breath that came from a body responding to stimulation it had no history with.

The tunic came free. I dropped it on the floor and looked down.

Without the leather. Without the binding. Without any armour between my eyes and the truth. The breasts were //there//.

Small. Still small. But no longer the ambiguous "puffiness" I had tried to dismiss a fortnight ago. They had shape now — a distinct, rounded shape, each one a soft mound of tissue approximately the size of my cupped palm. The skin was smooth, unbroken, paler than the surrounding chest where the sun had never touched because the tissue beneath was new — grown in darkness, beneath leather and linen, a secret the body was keeping from the sun.

The nipples sat at the centre of each mound. Larger than they had been. The areolae — that darker circle of skin surrounding the nub — had widened from the tight, flat discs of the male chest to something broader, softer, the colour deepening from pale pink toward a dusky rose. The nipples themselves were raised, prominent, and as I watched, the cool air of the chamber caused them to tighten — //harden// — the flesh puckering, the nub standing erect.

I watched my own nipples harden with a fascination that bordered on horror. This was a thing //women's// bodies did. A thing I had seen on women — on Maera, once, when a cold draught caught her thin sleeping shift and I had glanced and looked away, blushing. Now I was watching it happen on //my// chest, to nipples that were //mine//, and the body's casual, automatic response to cold air felt like a betrayal so intimate it belonged in a confession.

Trousers next. I unlaced them and pushed them down — over the hips that caught them, required the shimmy, the tug, the cursed wriggling that had become necessary — and stepped free.

Naked. Completely, entirely, devastatingly naked.

I forced myself to look.

[[The body in the mirror|warrior_queen_day_1_12]]


:: warrior_queen_day_1_12

The full-length mirror in the bath chamber had been my enemy for weeks. I avoided it. Angled past it. Dressed and undressed with my back to it. But Aldric's words sat in my skull like a blade — //built upon a frame that no longer exists// — and I owed the truth at least a glance.

